---
/**
 * PageShell - the shell every composed page wears, in one of four languages.
 *
 * The shell is the same object four times. It prints a declaration strip, then a
 * grid whose tracks are the language's, then a foot. What changes between the
 * languages is *which tracks exist and what they are called*, and that is
 * decided in `Language.ts` - this file only lays them out.
 *
 *   reading     rail | desk | paper | desk | margin
 *   workspace   rail | panes (the default slot holds a `ShellPanes`)
 *   audit       ledger | matrix | wells
 *   plate       head over a full-bleed plate | caption channel
 *
 * ─── READING: THE PAPER IS THE INVARIANT ─────────────────────────────────
 * The reading language is the one that is a document, so its grid is the one
 * that puts the paper in the middle and the rails at the page's edges: the rail
 * on the left, the register on the right, and a `1fr` desk on either side of the
 * plate. `--Measure` is the plate (72-88ch, fluid on the window) and the shell
 * widens to `--ContainerReading` so the plate can reach its full width with both
 * rails still outside it. A page that is 72ch wide on a 2560px screen has not
 * used the screen; a page that stretches prose to 160ch has stopped being a
 * document.
 *
 * The margin has THREE STATES and ONE SLOT: a sticky column at the right edge,
 * a horizontal bar pinned under the header (below 84rem of desk, where the plate
 * and both rails cannot share the width), and the stacked section the narrow
 * arm of every language prints. The bar is `order: -1` inside a wrapping flex
 * row - a state of this component, not a second markup - so every fact, clause
 * and note is present in all three, and the reader never meets two versions of
 * the same register.
 *
 * ─── THE DECLARATION STRIP ────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Every page on the site opens with one printed record, in two rows:
 *
 *   row 1  # LAYOUT / READING␁GIVES: RAIL␁MEASURE␁MARGIN␁FOR: THE LEXICON   # READING_09
 *   row 2  ↳ a rail (the document's own contents, or the set it belongs to)
 *          ↳ a measure (72-88ch of prose, or `full` for a page that also carries grids)
 *          ↳ a margin (the notes, the facts, the aside) - or nothing, and then
 *            the measure takes the rail's side
 *
 * Row 1 is the *declaration*: the `#` in the accent, `LAYOUT`, the slash and the
 * language's name in ink at the sub tier, its facts as `KEY:`/value pairs at the
 * meta tier separated by U+2001 (labels mute, values ink), and the page's own
 * stamp at the meta tier, tracked and mute, pinned to the row's end by the grid.
 * It is one line while the container can hold it, and head-and-stamp over facts
 * when it cannot - never three fragments, and never a stamp adrift in the middle
 * of the line. It is the line a reader scans.
 *
 * Row 2 is the *gloss*: the same three things as prose, at the body tier, one
 * proposition per item under a `↳`. The three clauses are a set - the tracks the
 * language gives, in the order it gives them - so they print as three columns at
 * a wide container and as a stack with the marks in a gutter column below it,
 * never as one run-together sentence. The shell prints `Spec.declares` verbatim:
 * the register owns the sentence, the shell owns the shape.
 *
 * The split is the whole point. Facts and explanation on one line compete, and
 * at one tier a right-flushed paragraph of clauses reads as a wall of small
 * capitals rather than as a declaration with a gloss. Two rows, three tiers, and
 * the hierarchy is ink, tracking and space - never weight.
 *
 * A page that is one artifact prints `DECLARES: NOTHING` and no tracks, because
 * that is what is true.
 *
 * ─── CONTAINER QUERIES, NOT VIEWPORT QUERIES ──────────────────────────────
 * The shell is a query container (`container: shell / inline-size`). Every track
 * decision below is a `@container shell (...)` rule, so a shell inside a narrower
 * slot re-composes instead of overflowing - which is what makes it possible to
 * put a reading shell inside a workspace pane, or a plate inside an audit, and
 * have both behave.
 *
 * ─── WHAT IT DOES NOT DO ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * - It renders no `<main>`: `Layout/Base.astro` owns the document's one
 *   `<main id="main-content">`, and a second one is a duplicate landmark.
 * - It renders no `<h1>`: the page's head does, once.
 * - It does not decide the language. `LanguageOf(route)` does, and `Base.astro`
 *   writes the same answer on `<html>`, so the frame's running head and the
 *   shell's grid cannot disagree.
 * - It paints no surface of its own. The field is creme; the tracks are creme;
 *   a white box inside a track is a Record block's business, never the shell's.
 *
 * No script and no hydration: a shell is structure.
 */
import { LanguageOf, Languages } from "./Language.js";

import type { LayoutAnswer } from "./Language.js";

interface Props {
	/**
	 * The shell's own record marker, pinned to the end of the declaration row -
	 * e.g. `# READING_01`. A page writes its own, because only the page knows
	 * which reading of the language it is.
	 */
	index?: string;

	/**
	 * The language. Omitted, the shell reads the route's filing - the normal
	 * case. Set, it is an *inner* shell: a plate inside a reading page, a
	 * reading shell inside a workspace pane. The page's own declaration is still
	 * the register's, and the inner one is labelled as such by `label`.
	 */
	language?: LayoutAnswer;

	/**
	 * What this shell is for, printed as the declaration row's second fact:
	 * `FOR: THE SALE LETTER`.
	 */
	label?: string;

	/**
	 * The reading measure. `prose` caps the main track at `--Measure` (the plate,
	 * 72-88ch); `full` leaves the body inside it open for a page that also
	 * carries grids. Ignored by the languages that do not read at a measure.
	 */
	measure?: "prose" | "full";

	/** The rail's accessible name. A rail without one is a list, not a landmark. */
	railLabel?: string;

	/** The margin's accessible name, when the margin is a landmark of its own. */
	marginLabel?: string;

	/** Extra classes from the page. */
	class?: string;
}

const {
	index,
	language,
	label,
	measure = "prose",
	railLabel,
	marginLabel,
	class: ClassName,
} = Astro.props;

const Spec = language ? Languages[language] : LanguageOf(Astro.url.pathname);

const HasRail = Astro.slots.has("rail");
const HasMargin = Astro.slots.has("margin");
const HasHead = Astro.slots.has("head");
const HasFoot = Astro.slots.has("foot");

/**
 * The declaration row's first fact. A language names the tracks it gives; the
 * one-artifact page has none to name and says so, rather than printing an empty
 * value where a reader expects three words.
 */
const GivesKey = Spec.key === "record" ? "DECLARES:" : "GIVES:";
const GivesValue = Spec.key === "record" ? "NOTHING" : Spec.gives;

/**
 * The tracks this shell actually gave, as one register value.
 *
 * The declaration's `GIVES:` prints what the LANGUAGE offers; this prints what
 * the page took, counted from the slots that are present - `RAIL␁PAPER␁MARGIN`,
 * `PAPER␁MARGIN`, `RAIL␁PAPER`. The two are not the same fact: a reading page
 * with no rail is still a reading page, and the strip said `GIVES: RAIL MEASURE
 * MARGIN` over a page that had one of the three. Printed only when a side track
 * is present, because `PAPER` alone is not news.
 */
const Tracks = [
	HasRail ? "RAIL" : null,
	"PAPER",
	HasMargin ? "MARGIN" : null,
]
	.filter((Track): Track is string => Track !== null)
	.join("\u2001");

const HasSideTracks = HasRail || HasMargin;
---

<div
	class:list={["PageShell", ClassName]}
	data-layout={Spec.key}
	data-measure={measure}
>
	<div class="PageShell__declaration">
		<p class="PageShell__record">
			<span class="PageShell__mark" aria-hidden="true">
				#
			</span>
			<span class="PageShell__word">LAYOUT</span>
			<span class="PageShell__slash" aria-hidden="true">
				/
			</span>
			<span class="PageShell__name">{Spec.name}</span>
		</p>

		<dl class="PageShell__facts">
			<div class="PageShell__fact">
				<dt class="PageShell__key">{GivesKey}</dt>
				<dd class="PageShell__tracks">{GivesValue}</dd>
			</div>

			{label ? (
				<div class="PageShell__fact">
					<dt class="PageShell__key">FOR:</dt>
					<dd class="PageShell__label">{label}</dd>
				</div>
			) : null}

			{HasSideTracks ? (
				<div class="PageShell__fact PageShell__fact--tracks">
					<dt class="PageShell__key">TRACKS:</dt>
					<dd class="PageShell__tracks PageShell__tracks--given">{Tracks}</dd>
				</div>
			) : null}
		</dl>

		{index ? <p class="PageShell__index">{index}</p> : null}

		<ol class="PageShell__declares">
			{Spec.declares.map((Clause) => (
				<li class="PageShell__clause">
					<span class="PageShell__clause-mark" aria-hidden="true">
						↳
					</span>
					<span class="PageShell__clause-text">{Clause}</span>
				</li>
			))}
		</ol>
	</div>

	{HasHead ? (
		<div class="PageShell__head">
			<slot name="head" />
		</div>
	) : null}

	<div class="PageShell__grid">
		{HasRail ? (
			<aside
				class="PageShell__rail"
				aria-label={railLabel ?? `${Spec.name} rail`}
			>
				<slot name="rail" />
			</aside>
		) : null}

		<div class="PageShell__main">
			<slot />
		</div>

		{HasMargin ? (
			<div
				class="PageShell__margin"
				aria-label={marginLabel ?? `${Spec.name} margin`}
			>
				<slot name="margin" />
			</div>
		) : null}
	</div>

	{HasFoot ? (
		<div class="PageShell__foot">
			<slot name="foot" />
		</div>
	) : null}
</div>
